<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ukrainisation</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1923–1933</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Ukrainization</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ukrainisation was the Soviet policy of the 1920s and early 1930s that promoted the Ukrainian language and ethnic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainians">Ukrainians</a> in the schools, press, culture and administration of the Ukrainian SSR. It was the Ukrainian form of korenizatsiia, the Bolshevik policy of rooting Soviet power among the non-Russian nationalities. Stalin reversed it from 1932–1933, and many of its leaders were purged; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mykola-skrypnyk">Mykola Skrypnyk</a> took his own life in 1933.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ukrainisation.jpg" alt="Ukrainisation" width="320" height="265" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Johann Homann · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ukrania_quae_et_Terra_Cosaccorum_cum_vicinis_Walachiae,_Moldoviae,_Johann_Baptiste_Homann_(Nuremberg,_1720).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
